+/* ------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
+/* Q8_0 weights, consumed exactly where they lie                             */
+/* ------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
+/*
+ * A GGUF q8_0 row is K/32 blocks of 34 bytes: an f16 scale then 32 signed
+ * bytes. The dequantising cache every engine starts with expands that to f32
+ * before the first token -- 3.76x the bytes and a full pass over the model --
+ * and on a 1.2 GiB checkpoint that is 4.4 GiB of resident weights that will
+ * not fit a 3.9 GiB box at all.
+ *
+ * This kernel reads the blocks as they are. Per block it widens 32 signed
+ * bytes to two vectors of 16 f32 lanes (one vpmovsxbd + vcvtdq2ps each), does
+ * the FMAs against A, and folds the block scale in once per block rather than
+ * once per weight:
+ *
+ *     C[m,n] = sum_b d_b * ( sum_{i<32} A[m,32b+i] * q_b[i] )
+ *
+ * The scale multiply moves off the 32 FMAs and onto one, so the arithmetic is
+ * the same as the dequantising dot and the memory traffic is 4x smaller. That
+ * ratio is the entire point on a prefill, which is bound by how fast B can be
+ * streamed rather than by flops.
+ *
+ * Rows of the output are blocked 4 at a time because four rows share one
+ * reading of B -- the same reason the f32 tile does it.
+ */
+
+/* F16 -> f32, matching the conversion the dequantising paths use. */
+static inline float q8_0_block_scale(const uint8_t *blk)
+{
+    uint16_t h;
+    std::memcpy(&h, blk, sizeof(h));
+    return vnpu_f16_to_float(h);
+}
